  "_doc": "Ship classes of the war (docs/GUERRA.md). Read by the battle at startup over the table compiled in (Source/ASTRA/AstraWarClassesData.inl, regenerated with `tools/war.py embed`). Frames: X forward, Y starboard, Z up. Sections: 0 bow, 1 mid, 2 stern. radius: the farthest end of the hull from the mesh's origin (m); tier: how big it counts (3 capital ship, 2 cruiser, 1 destroyer or freighter, 0 smaller) for what depends on class size; hull_m: the mesh's own measures (x from the stern end to the bow end, width, height: the box a shot strikes); cuts_x_m: where the break-up pieces are cut (x of the bow cut and the stern cut), the bow piece from the first cut to the bow, the middle between them, the stern from the second cut to the stern (from art/export/ships_v3/manifest.json). Facings: [bow, stern, port, starboard, dorsal, ventral]. Systems: engines, sensors, hangar, bridge, reactor (the section each sits in). Mounts: kind rail|laser, dir (unit, ship frame), arc (half-angle, degrees), barrels (slugs a shot), section. BATTAGLIA-3: a rail mount fires its barrels once every `cd` seconds (each mount in its own time: a ship's fire is a stream, not a volley), a slug flies at 12 km/s and goes where the fire control's error puts it (docs/GUERRA.md, `gunnery`: track_mrad is the milliradians of tracking error of its sensors, the miss at a range is that times the range; disp_mrad the scatter of the guns); lasers are light (no flight, but the same tracking error, and `falloff` is what a beam still carries at the end of its range). range_km: the band of distances its armament likes (the AI keeps its fights there).",
